WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.127
Welcome to Module 6.

00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:07.307
In this module, we are going to move from writing small pieces of Python code toward organizing larger

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:12.957
Python programs in a cleaner and more reusable way.

00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:18.137
So far, we have practiced variables, control flow, functions, lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets.

00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:22.042
Those are the building blocks of Python.

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:24.897
But as your programs grow, you also need structure.

00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:31.872
If all of your code stays in one long file, it becomes harder to read, harder to test,

00:00:31.872 --> 00:00:33.702
harder to fix, and harder to reuse later.

00:00:33.702 --> 00:00:38.992
That is why this module focuses on modules, packages, and object-oriented programming.

00:00:38.992 --> 00:00:45.967
These ideas help us organize code into smaller pieces that have clear responsibilities.

00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:49.942
We will begin with imports and built-in modules.

00:00:49.942 --> 00:00:56.447
Then we will create our own small module, discuss packages and libraries, and move into classes, objects,

00:00:56.447 --> 00:00:57.447
methods, and self.

00:00:57.447 --> 00:01:03.607
By the end of this module, you should be able to explain when a function, dictionary, module, package,

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:06.697
or class is the right tool for a simple analytics problem.

00:01:06.697 --> 00:01:13.557
As you go through the lesson, do not worry about memorizing every detail immediately.

00:01:13.557 --> 00:01:20.080
Focus on the main idea: organized code is easier to understand, easier to maintain, and easier to reuse.
